Y.M. &ldquo;Because its performance is not personal. It is the result of the law of
                construction. It is not a <i>merit</i> that it does the things which it is set
                to do&mdash;it can&rsquo;t <i>help</i> doing them.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Spontaneously? No. And <i>you did not form that one</i>; your machinery
                did it for you&mdash;automatically and instantly, without reflection or the
                need of it.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Not a shred of it. <i>It grew out of this talk with me</i>. But for that
                it would not have occurred to you. No man ever originates anything. All his
                thoughts, all his impulses, come <i>from the outside</i>.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;And so we are mere machines! And machines may not boast, nor feel proud of
                their performance, nor claim personal merit for it, nor applause and praise. It
                is an infamous doctrine.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;There isn&rsquo;t any. In the world&rsquo;s view he is a worthier man than
                he was before, but <i>he</i> didn&rsquo;t achieve the change&mdash;the merit of
                it is not his.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Because it puts him in the attitude of always looking out for his own
                comfort and advantage; whereas an unselfish man often does a thing solely for
                another person&rsquo;s good when it is a positive disadvantage to himself.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;No. There is <i>no</i> act, large or small, fine or mean, which springs
                from any motive but the one&mdash;the necessity of appeasing and contenting
                one&rsquo;s own spirit.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Oh, a million unnoticed influences&mdash;for good or bad: influences which
                work without rest during every waking moment of a man&rsquo;s life, from cradle
                to grave.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;He does seven-tenths of the act because it gratifies <i>him</i> to gratify
                his mother. Throw the bulk of advantage the other way and the good boy would
                not do the act. He <i>must</i> obey the iron law. None can escape it.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;You needn&rsquo;t mention it, it is a waste of time. It is no matter about
                the bad boy&rsquo;s act. Whatever it was, he had a spirit-contenting reason for
                it. Otherwise you have been misinformed, and he didn&rsquo;t do it.&rdquo;

&ldquo;<i>I believed, and was happy in it; you have taken my belief away, and
                    my comfort. Now I have nothing left, and I die miserable; for the things which
                    you have told me do not take the place of that which I have lost</i>.&rdquo;

&ldquo;<i>It was wrong&mdash;I see it now; but I was only trying to do him
                    good. In my view he was in error; it seemed my duty to teach him the
                    truth</i>.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Call it what you please, it is to me a case of <i>awakened conscience</i>.
                That awakened conscience could never get itself into that species of trouble
                again. A cure like that is a <i>permanent</i> cure.&rdquo;

&ldquo;<i>I believed, and was happy in it; you have taken my belief away, and
                    my comfort. Now I have nothing left, and I die miserable; for the things which
                    you have told me do not take the place of that which I have lost</i>.&rdquo;

&ldquo;<i>My child is forever lost, and my heart is broken. How could you do
                    this cruel thing? We had done you no harm, but only kindness; we made our house
                    your home, you were welcome to all we had, and this is our reward</i>.&rdquo;

&ldquo;<i>It was wrong&mdash;I see it now; but I was only trying to do him
                    good. In my view he was in error; it seemed my duty to teach him the
                    truth</i>.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;It was I that moved you to it. That is to say an <i>outside influence</i>
                moved you to it&mdash;not one that originated in your head. Will you try to
                keep that in mind and not forget it?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Because by and by in one of our talks, I wish to further impress upon you
                that neither you, nor I, nor any man ever originates a thought in his own head.
                <i>The utterer of a thought always utters a second-hand one</i>.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;The young brother&rsquo;s education&mdash;well, an extinguishing blight
                fell upon that happy dream, and he had to go to sawing wood to support the old
                father, or something like that?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;No&mdash;at least, not yet. But take the case of servant&mdash;tipping in
                Europe. You pay the <i>hotel</i> for service; you owe the servants
                <i>nothing</i>, yet you pay them besides. Doesn&rsquo;t that defeat it?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Very well, then. As I understand it, it isn&rsquo;t really compassion nor
                yet duty that moves you to pay the tax, and it isn&rsquo;t the <i>amount</i> of
                the tax that annoys you. Yet <i>something</i> annoys you. What is it?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;It has quite a noble look&mdash;taking so much pains and using up so much
                valuable time in order to be just and fair to a poor servant to whom you owe
                nothing, but who needs money and is ill paid.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;It is my belief that you have <i>not</i> been concerning yourself in
                guessing out his just dues, but only in ciphering out what would <i>content</i>
                him. And I think you have a self-deluding reason for that.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Yes. No man performs a duty for mere duty&rsquo;s sake; the act must
                content his spirit <i>first</i>. He must feel better for <i>doing</i> the duty
                than he would for shirking it. Otherwise he will not do it.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Take the case of a clergyman of stainless private morals who votes for a
                thief for public office, on his own party&rsquo;s ticket, and against an honest
                man on the other ticket.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;He has to content his spirit. He has no public morals; he has no private
                ones, where his party&rsquo;s prosperity is at stake. He will always be true to
                his make and training.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Merely the laying of traps for people. Traps baited with <i>initiatory
                    impulses toward high ideals. </i>It is what the tract-distributor does. It is
                what the missionary does. It is what governments ought to do.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Unquestionably. There is no other source for any impulse. <i>Secondarily
                </i>you made preparation to save the girl, but <i>primarily </i>its object was
                to save yourself, by contenting the Master.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;(<i>After a reflective pause) </i>Temperament. Well, I see one must allow
                for temperament. It is a large factor, sure enough. My mother is thoughtful,
                and not hot-tempered. When I was dressed I went to her room; she was not there;
                I called, she answered from the bathroom. I heard the water running. I
                inquired. She answered, without temper, that Jane had forgotten her bath, and
                she was preparing it herself. I offered to ring, but she said, &ldquo;No,
                don&rsquo;t do that; it would only distress her to be confronted with her
                lapse, and would be a rebuke; she doesn&rsquo;t deserve that&mdash;she is not
                to blame for the tricks her memory serves her.&rdquo; I say&mdash;has my mother
                an Interior Master?&mdash;and where was he?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;If we grant, for the sake of argument, that your scheme and the other
                schemes aim at and produce the same result&mdash;<i>right living&mdash;</i>has
                yours an advantage over the others?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Is that an advantage? Is it an advantage to live a lofty life for a mean
                reason? In the other cases he lives the lofty life under the <i>impression
                </i>that he is living for a lofty reason. Is not that an advantage?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;But anyway, he is obliged to do a duke&rsquo;s part; he puts his hand in
                his pocket and does his benevolences on as big a scale as he can stand, and
                that benefits the community.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;It is my opinion that under your scheme of a man&rsquo;s doing a good deed
                for his <i>own </i>sake first-off, instead of first for the <i>good
                    deed&rsquo;s </i>sake, no man would ever do one.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;The cabin of the old negro woman who used to nurse me when I was a child
                and who saved my life once at the risk of her own, was burned last night, and
                she came mourning this morning, and pleading for money to build another one.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Of course I was; for if I hadn&rsquo;t had the horse I should have been
                incapable, and my <i>mother </i>would have captured the chance to set old Sally
                up.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Then you believe that such tendency toward doing good as is in men&rsquo;s
                hearts would not be diminished by the removal of the delusion that good deeds
                are done primarily for the sake of No. 2 instead of for the sake of No. 1?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;<i>Diligently train your ideals upward and still upward toward a summit
                    where you will find your chiefest pleasure in conduct which, while contenting
                    you, will be sure to confer benefits upon your neighbor and the community.</i>&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;If I conclude to rob a person, I am not the <i>originator </i>of the idea,
                but it comes in from the <i>outside</i>? I see him handling money&mdash;for
                instance&mdash;and <i>that </i>moves me to the crime?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Now, dreams&mdash;but we will examine that later. Meantime, did you try
                commanding your mind to wait for orders from you, and not do any thinking on
                its own hook?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Your dreaming mind originates the scheme, consistently and artistically
                develops it, and carries the little drama creditably through&mdash;all without
                help or suggestion from you?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Are you determined to go on believing in these insanities? Would you go on
                believing in them in the face of able arguments backed by collated facts and
                instances?&rdquo;

Young Man. &ldquo;It is odious. Those drunken theories of yours, advanced a while
                ago&mdash;concerning the rat and all that&mdash;strip Man bare of all his
                dignities, grandeurs, sublimities.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Granting, then, that dumb animals are able to think upon a low plane, is
                there any that can think upon a high one? Is there one that is well up toward
                man?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Yes, go back to the ant, the creature that&mdash;as you seem to
                think&mdash;sweeps away the last vestige of an intellectual frontier between
                man and the Unrevealed.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;It would elevate the savage if he had it. But let us look further before
                we decide. The ant has soldiers&mdash;battalions, regiments, armies; and they
                have their appointed captains and generals, who lead them to battle.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;I have to concede it. It was not a result of habit; it has all the look of
                reflection, thought, putting this and that together, as you phrase it. I
                believe it was thought.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;We have come a good way. As a result&mdash;as I understand it&mdash;I am
                required to concede that there is absolutely no intellectual frontier
                separating Man and the Unrevealed Creatures?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Then man and the other animals are all alike, as to mental machinery, and
                there isn&rsquo;t any difference of any stupendous magnitude between them,
                except in quality, not in kind.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Very well, let them have what they&rsquo;ve got, and welcome; there is
                still a wall, and a lofty one. They haven&rsquo;t got the Moral Sense; we have
                it, and it lifts us immeasurably above them.&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Now look here&mdash;let&rsquo;s call a halt. I have stood the other
                infamies and insanities and that is enough; I am not going to have man and the
                other animals put on the same level morally.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;The one implies untrammeled power to <i>act </i>as you please, the other
                implies nothing beyond a mere <i>mental process: </i>the critical ability to
                determine which of two things is nearest right and just.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;His temperament and training will decide what he shall do, and he will do
                it; he cannot help himself, he has no authority over the matter. Wasn&rsquo;t
                it right for David to go out and slay Goliath?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;You know that a born coward&rsquo;s make and temperament would be an
                absolute and insurmountable bar to his ever essaying such a thing, don&rsquo;t
                you?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Yes. The Master in you requires that in <i>all </i>cases you shall content
                his <i>spirit </i>&mdash;that alone. He never requires anything else, he never
                interests himself in any other matter.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;It is that mysterious autocrat, lodged in a man, which compels the man to
                content its desires. It may be called the Master Passion&mdash;the hunger for
                Self-Approval.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;It is a <i>colorless </i>force seated in the man&rsquo;s moral
                constitution. Let us call it an instinct&mdash;a blind, unreasoning instinct,
                which cannot and does not distinguish between good morals and bad ones, and
                cares nothing for results to the man provided its own contentment be secured;
                and it will <i>always </i>secure that.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;He had to satisfy his master&mdash;that is to say, his temperament, his
                Spiritual Appetite&mdash;and it preferred books to money. Are there other
                cases?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;Well, lying abed resting up, two days and nights, I have thought over all
                these talks, and passed them carefully in review. With this result: that...
                that... are you intending to publish your notions about Man some day?&rdquo;

Y.M. &ldquo;The matter of publishing or withholding is still in your Master&rsquo;s
                hands. If some day an outside influence shall determine him to publish, he will
                give the order, and it will be obeyed.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Who devised the law by which it automatically hammers out of a piano an
                elaborate piece of music, without error, while the man is thinking about
                something else, or talking to a friend?&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;That is correct. If a man is born with an unhappy temperament, nothing can
                make him happy; if he is born with a happy temperament, nothing can make him
                unhappy.&rdquo;

O.M. &ldquo;Now you are speaking hastily. It shows that you have not studiously
                examined the facts. Of all your intimates, which one is the happiest?
                Isn&rsquo;t it Burgess?&rdquo;
